## Development network · `sec:devnet:record`

**Decision (Development realization)** · `dec:devnet:elements` The repository ships a declared development profile named {{ profile.name }}: a standalone Elements chain carrying its own test asset, whose blocks satisfy a published signing challenge. The repository's development-network operator runs the signer, node and faucet; it publishes the challenge, chain parameters, asset issuance and run identity, and makes the evidence available through (`rec:api:decision`). Tokens have no promised market value, redemption or production backing; their declared test-unit cost and limited release schedule are described in (`cav:devnet:assurance`). This profile exists only for development and tests, never as a production deployment. It supplies test inputs below (`conv:vision:burn-realization`), without changing (`req:vision:burn-boundary`) or asserting the external assurance the implementation imports.

**Decision (Release policy)** · `dec:devnet:release` {{ require(profile.release_line == '29.4' and profile.initial_tag != '', 'development chain release line and initial tag must be declared') }}Use the {{ profile.release_line }} release line of Elements from {{ profile.repository }}, with {{ profile.initial_tag }} as the selected starting tag. Track the latest published release or release candidate in that line by explicit dependency review and a new gate run, recording the actual public tag and runtime version in the deployment declaration. A later line requires its own reviewed change. This follows the early-integration purpose of (`dec:toolchain:compiler`) and (`dec:storage:postgresql`), while pinning one admitted binary and parameter set for the life of a chain run: an automatic binary change must not revise chain history. Before 1.0, reset into a new declared run rather than migrate an old store or silently exchange its chain (`rule:compatibility:pre-one`).

**Rule (Signed Elements chain)** · `rule:devnet:chain` The word signet here requires an Elements asset chain secured by its declared block-signing challenge. Elements exposes signed-block parameters con_signed_blocks and signblockscript separately from its inherited signet and signetchallenge options; the presence of both options is not evidence that one launch mode combines them. The implementation gate must demonstrate asset issuance, transfer, destruction and rejection of an invalid block signature in the selected signed Elements mode. An inherited Bitcoin-only signet, an unsigned regression chain or an always-satisfied challenge is not a substitute. Publish the chosen public parameters and verification rules; unsupported combinations hold the profile rather than weakening this decision. No launch configuration is claimed by this record.

**Model (Burn event and address)** · `model:devnet:burn` A qualifying event is a settled on-chain destruction output of the declared test asset with a positive, explicit integer amount and an independently checkable unspendable script. The output commits one permanent target burn address in its signed transaction before broadcast; missing, ambiguous or wrong-run target encodings are refused. The address is the declared chain-run identity paired with an opaque permanent name selected for the consumer's authenticated binding; it is not the destruction outpoint and makes no assertion about a person or wallet owner. Each destruction outpoint identifies one evidence event and contributes at most once to its committed target. Multiple fresh destructions may increase that target's amount; no event or already recorded amount may be moved, merged or reassigned to another address. Do not infer attribution from a change address, wallet label or mutable receipt. destroyamount illustrates on-chain destruction, but its ordinary null-data output alone supplies no target binding and its wallet comment is not on-chain evidence. The profile's target-bearing constructor and decoder require the evidence in (`amend:devnet:burn-targets`). Fees, ordinary transfers, grants, other assets and unconfirmed outputs contribute no burn amount.

**Rule (Mathematical interface)** · `rule:devnet:burn-map` {{ require(profile.initial_units > 0 and profile.units_per_token == 100000000 and profile.unit_cost_test_units == 1, 'test burn units and finite supply must be explicit') }}The test burn-amount map at an address is the exact sum of distinct qualifying pinned destructions represented in the recognized marker policy's accepted sets and committed to that address, zero when none have been accepted, with {{ profile.units_per_token | number }} units per displayed token. The opaque address space is defined by (`model:devnet:burn`). Declare a bootstrap capacity bound of {{ profile.initial_units | number }} units, backed by a fixed initial supply of that many units, no subsequent issuance of that asset, no reachable reissuance authority and no peg or subsidy minting it; the faucet opens at a published boundary strictly after the run starts, following a declared nonempty pre-opening interval. That opening is the external-acquisition boundary for this test accounting. The entire initial test-asset supply is held for the faucet, the only source of newly distributed test asset; no operator distribution, fee payout of undistributed test asset or other route may bypass (`rule:devnet:faucet-limits`). Fee funding must not create such a route. Transfers of already distributed asset do not create supply. Declare a unit-cost projection of {{ profile.unit_cost_test_units | number }} test-asset unit irreversibly consumed per burn unit, at the profile's published terms when destruction occurs. This equality follows from choosing the same test-asset and burn unit and counting destruction as the sole proportional outlay, not from a universal lower bound. Publish any transaction or recording-event cost separately with its denomination and terms; never fold it into the amount-proportional projection. The published faucet schedule bounds release into circulation, not the later rate at which holders burn accumulated balances. These are the four test counterparts of ``def:comparator:burn-interface``; depth, chain provenance and operator-bound evidence remain disclosures, not extra mathematical inputs. A finite faucet quota is not proof of a finite supply or bootstrap bound.

**Caveat (Economic assurance)** · `cav:devnet:assurance` The fixed supply and sole-source faucet schedule provide a declared supply curve in test-asset units: recipient and settled-window quotas bound release, and every qualifying burn consumes a resource whose initial quantity and release rate are known and limited (`rule:devnet:burn-map`), (`rule:devnet:faucet-limits`). The curve is a ceiling on release from fixed stock, not a promised quantity or a price-demand relation. This exercises scarcity, exhaustion and acquisition opportunities in the schedule's own units, together with the published unit-cost projection: each burn unit requires the irreversible outlay of its backing test-asset unit at the terms effective when it is destroyed. The faucet charges no monetary purchase price; its quota is an upper bound on access, not a positive monetary acquisition-cost lower bound or a promise that a grant is available. The paper's ``cond:comparator:burn-costly-append-only-record`` and ``cond:comparator:burn-unit-cost-projection`` price destruction at the realization's own terms and do not demand an external market or a particular demand curve; the test-unit accounting can therefore exercise those clauses without claiming an external price. The schedule neither determines demand nor establishes a market exchange rate, monetary opportunity cost or human acquisition limit: addresses are opaque, recipients may share an actor, and already distributed tokens may be transferred or accumulated. Such external economic conclusions do not follow from the supply schedule. Chain conservation, exclusive distribution, destruction provenance, settlement assumptions and any conditional operator bound still require published evidence, including a proved bound if an operational role accrues burn amount, or an explained statement of absence otherwise; declared quotas alone prove none of those chain facts. Outputs model this controlled realization and carry its assumptions and test denomination beside every profile and package, never production assurance. Numerical or replay success does not discharge the realization evidence required by ``subsec:deployment:realization-obligations``.

**Rule (Settlement and provenance)** · `rule:devnet:settlement` {{ require(profile.settlement_blocks > 0 and profile.snapshot_buffer_blocks > profile.settlement_blocks, 'sample lag must exceed declared settlement depth') }}Pin chain evidence for an event only after {{ profile.settlement_blocks | number }} successor blocks under the declared signed-chain rule; recognize the final marker with a buffer of {{ profile.snapshot_buffer_blocks | number }} blocks. Its lineage and fixed interpretation must meet the same declared depth and finality assumptions. Pinning an event alone does not accept it into the network snapshot: derive that snapshot from the complete accepted sets under (`rule:design:marker-recognition`) and (`alg:design:burn-snapshot`). Before an event pins, its contribution stays zero and the address retains its previously pinned contributions. Carry raw transaction/output evidence, asset issuance lineage, inclusion and header/signature evidence, run identity, effective parameters, sampled chain position and the original declarations, so a reader can recompute rather than trust an RPC balance. This profile supports explicit asset/amount outputs; an opaque commitment without the admitted public evidence is unsupported, never guessed or totalled as a clear amount. A fork before the pinned floor may change pending evidence; a conflicting history beyond that floor is a realization fault which stops new admission/publication for the run, never a downward edit of pinned amounts. Signer trust and the no-deep-revision promise remain operational assumptions, not a cryptographic finality theorem. External transaction and block identifiers locate chain evidence only; they do not replace structural act identity or select a network verdict. Preserve the faulty run's evidence; a permitted reset starts a distinct run under (`rule:compatibility:pre-one`).

**Rule (Custody and configuration)** · `rule:devnet:custody` The development-network operator owns availability, signer authorization, wallet funding and recovery. Its signet signing key, faucet spending keys, RPC credentials, wallet unlock material and complete private RPC reachability details exist in the implementation only in the secret carriers of (`dec:observe:secrets`), from acquisition to explicit use; none enters tracked files. Retained client configuration and errors must preserve that boundary under (`inv:observe:secret-boundary`) before integration is admitted. Node custody must meet its own reviewed operational boundary; a Rust carrier does not prove a separate daemon's key handling. Public chain parameters, asset supply, rates and evidence live in the database or public inputs; node-side private RPC settings are secret-owned database material under (`rule:storage:configuration`). pn-wallet receives its private wallet/RPC access through the deployment's secret bootstrap boundary and needs no client database or journal file; the Elements wallet retains its spend authority and recovery state under (`rule:harness:wallet-recovery`). The external node's restricted operational configuration is the deployment's responsibility. All public requests, transactions, results and errors are observed in full; secret observations show only their role and redaction marker, with no content filtering (`rule:observe:privacy`). Only pn-wallet holds the Elements RPC integration, for both host and client callers; host supplies secret bootstrap through that boundary rather than retaining a second RPC client. Its node and client wallet contexts retain their distinct custody and authorization scopes.

**Signature (Faucet command)** · `sig:devnet:faucet` Expose faucet as a single-root GraphQL mutation under (`model:api:request`), carried in CBOR through (`proto:api:http`). Its variables name the declared development run, a public recipient destination, an exact positive unsigned amount in minimal units and a caller-authored request identifier. Validate the destination against the declared chain and normalize equivalent destination spellings to the same script before quota lookup; this profile requires an unblinded destination and explicit outputs. The result under (`model:api:response`) contains the run, request identifier, normalized recipient, exact amount, durable grant status, effective policy edition and, once known, transaction identity and output position; incomplete status says what remains unknown. Byte-valued identifiers use (`conv:api:scalars`), and binding receipt documents, when supplied, travel unchanged under (`inv:api:document-bytes`). A grant transfers existing test asset for a possible later burn; it is not destruction, a burn receipt, standing or admission. No wallet-admin or arbitrary RPC surface is exposed.

**Rule (Profile refusal)** · `rule:devnet:profile-guard` Both the API resolver and host grant operation check the active deployment profile and chain-run identity before reserving funds or contacting the wallet. In every non-development profile, faucet reports unsupported; it is not merely absent from introspection or hidden from a menu. A caller-supplied development flag cannot enable it. A reset invalidates old run-scoped requests and cannot replay them against the new wallet. Schema and profile evolution follow (`rule:compatibility:pre-one`); the faucet extends (`amend:api:development-faucet`) without changing transport semantics.

**Rule (Faucet budgets)** · `rule:devnet:faucet-limits` {{ require(profile.grant_units > 0 and profile.recipient_grants_per_window > 0 and profile.global_grants_per_window >= profile.recipient_grants_per_window and profile.window_blocks > 0 and profile.max_pending_grants > 0 and profile.fee_reserve_units > 0, 'faucet budgets must be finite positive declarations') }}This is the published release schedule for the sole source of newly distributed test asset, drawn only from the fixed supply in (`rule:devnet:burn-map`), with no reissuance. The initial profile gives exactly {{ profile.grant_units | number }} units per grant, also the per-request maximum; each normalized recipient may reserve {{ profile.recipient_grants_per_window | number }} grant per {{ profile.window_blocks | number }} settled blocks, and the entire faucet may reserve {{ profile.global_grants_per_window | number }} grants in that window. Thus the recipient and global amount budgets are respectively {{ (profile.recipient_grants_per_window * profile.grant_units) | number }} and {{ (profile.global_grants_per_window * profile.grant_units) | number }} units per window. Windows are fixed by the integer quotient of the pinned chain height by {{ profile.window_blocks | number }}; no pinned progress means no refill. Bound outstanding uncertain grants by {{ profile.max_pending_grants | number }} and retain at least {{ profile.fee_reserve_units | number }} units of the declared fee asset, in addition to a conservative fee reservation per transaction. Reservations, committed grants and uncertain broadcasts all consume their window allocation until proven not to have distributed asset; a retry consumes no second grant. Unused window allowance does not carry forward, and exhausting the fixed supply cannot be repaired by a refill or policy change. Public policy editions may change these values at a later boundary, without erasing already consumed window budgets or replenishing supply; publish the resulting piecewise schedule and preserve earlier terms. Global counters serialize all callers, so fresh addresses do not evade the aggregate bound; this is resource control, not a claim of human uniqueness. API body, cost, concurrency, response and deadline ceilings remain mandatory under (`rule:api:limits`).

**Rule (Grant outcomes and retries)** · `rule:devnet:grant-outcomes` Invalid amounts or destinations fail validation; a wrong profile or run reports unsupported. A reached quota or pending ceiling reports the named resource refusal and next eligible pinned window where known; insufficient spendable balance after reservations and fees reports faucet empty, with no grant. Node unavailability is service unavailable, and operational timeout or ambiguous broadcast yields an incomplete outcome, never mathematical FAIL or INDECISIVE (`rule:api:outcomes`). Bind each run/request identifier permanently to its typed recipient and amount: exact retries return stored status without consuming quota again, while conflicting reuse is refused. Atomically reserve quota and chosen wallet outpoints in PostgreSQL with a durable grant intent before signing or broadcasting. pn-wallet serializes wallet spending and returns selected outpoints to the host reservation path; an externally spent or otherwise unavailable outpoint requires reconciliation, not a claim that the database locked the chain. pn-wallet durably binds the request and chosen signed transaction in its wallet before broadcast under (`rule:harness:wallet-recovery`); recovery through pn-wallet queries or rebroadcasts those same bytes, never constructs a second payment merely because a response was lost. PostgreSQL and the chain are not one transaction: retain uncertain reservations until chain reconciliation establishes their disposition; never report a rollback simply from a timeout. A successful send is not settlement, and spent grants remain counted in their window even if later transferred or burned. Host owns PostgreSQL quota reservations and reconciled grant history, while all chain lookups, settlement checks and signing/broadcast operations go through pn-wallet. A reservation failure must prevent wallet broadcast; no database row substitutes for wallet transaction authority.

**Rule (History and observation)** · `rule:devnet:history` Under (`model:storage:scd2`), declarations, burn-evidence states and grant states have immutable payloads with append-only closures and successors, resolved at their own authoritative effective boundaries. Append pending, pinned, broadcast, settled or faulted transitions without overwriting the earlier state; simultaneous internal observations use a total storage sequence within the boundary rather than conflicting zero-length effective rows. Quota reservations and grant state commit atomically in PostgreSQL; external chain effects are reconciled as (`rule:devnet:grant-outcomes`) requires. A grant row never increments the burn map or the standing ledger. The later qualifying burn supplies provenance, but only its first representation in a recognized marker's accepted set supplies an increment to the burn carrier; charged ledger vintages retain their original samples. Trace all work under its repository labels, meter RPC and settlement work, and keep metric attributes bounded for cardinality under (`rec:observe:decision`). Claim labels are minted only by establishing tests under (`rec:testing:decision`).

**Gate (Development evidence)** · `gate:devnet:implementation` Before using the profile, run the relevant bite gate against a real Elements signed test-asset chain of the admitted release and the real PostgreSQL beta of (`gate:storage:evidence`). Record runtime versions and public run parameters; a missing node, wrong mode, invalid challenge or unavailable database fails the gate rather than skipping tests. Demonstrate the declared issuance ceiling and absence of mint paths, explicit destruction and exact units, duplicate exclusion, wrong-asset rejection, shallow reordering, pinned-floor fault handling, unchanged earlier SCD2 reads, non-development faucet refusal, concurrent recipient/global budgets, empty-wallet refusal, retry identity and recovery on both sides of broadcast. Independently decode chain fixtures and compare raw evidence, never use the same RPC summary as both answer and oracle. Test full public observation and secret-safe client/error paths. Placement and contributing bites are (`proposal:design:devnet-adapter`) and (`gate:design:devnet-bites`); unexecuted evidence remains (`open:design:devnet-evidence`). This record provisions nothing.

**Amendment (Permanent burn targets)** · `amend:devnet:burn-targets` The client workflows in (`sig:harness:clients`) require fresh destruction to fund a new or an existing permanent burn binding. This amends (`model:devnet:burn`), (`rule:devnet:burn-map`) and (`rule:devnet:settlement`): the prior address-per-outpoint choice prevented a fixed address from receiving another fresh burn, so outpoints now identify evidence events and the signed output commits the permanent target before broadcast. No old burn moves and no actor binding is rewritten. The target encoding, authenticated account-binding procedure and exact output constructor remain implementation obligations, with independent vectors, replay and tests for missing/changed targets, double counting, wrong runs and unauthorized rebinding before use (`rule:harness:burn-targets`). A post-hoc wallet comment or API assertion is never the target proof. This is a development-profile change under (`rule:compatibility:pre-one`), not a change to the paper's imported boundary or its economic-assurance limitation.

**Amendment (Controlled test supply)** · `amend:devnet:supply-schedule` The realization decision, burn map, assurance caveat and faucet budgets now state the fixed supply and exclusive release schedule together (`dec:devnet:elements`), (`rule:devnet:burn-map`), (`cav:devnet:assurance`), (`rule:devnet:faucet-limits`). Limited release and irreversible test-unit outlay let the profile exercise its own declared cost terms; free distribution alone does not negate that model. This replaces the blanket exclusion of cost modelling while keeping unproved chain facts, external economic conclusions and production assurance outside the claim. The profile guard and the imported burn boundary are unchanged.

**Amendment (Development wallet authority)** · `amend:devnet:wallet-authority` pn-wallet is the only Elements RPC integration and owns test-asset funding, target-committed burn construction, signing, broadcast and recovery (`dec:harness:client-packages`). Its Elements wallet is the source of truth for created and spent transactions; loss of an RPC reply triggers request-bound lookup, never another payment. The signed burn commitment includes the run/request association as well as the permanent target, and independent decoding checks both (`rule:harness:wallet-recovery`). This refines (`amend:devnet:burn-targets`) without moving old burn amount or treating a wallet comment as provenance. Wallet selection, keys, credentials and operating details follow (`rule:devnet:custody`); process-local secret carriers do not prove daemon custody. The node's faucet still keeps grant quotas and reconciled grant history under (`rule:devnet:grant-outcomes`) and (`rule:devnet:history`); those node records are not a client recovery store. Real-wallet restart, concurrent retry, incomplete history and lost-reply cases are required before client spending is supported. Host development burn inspection, faucet spending and settlement checks now call pn-wallet, replacing a separate host RPC integration. One integration is admitted once against its contract, with caller-specific authorization and independent evidence checks. The optional host development-network dependency and retained runtime profile guard are recorded in (`amend:decomposition:infrastructure`); replay remains offline.

**Amendment (Named realization obligations)** · `amend:devnet:burn-obligation-labels` (`cav:devnet:assurance`) addresses the released costliness and unit-cost requirements through their own labels, ``cond:comparator:burn-costly-append-only-record`` and ``cond:comparator:burn-unit-cost-projection``, instead of positions in the enclosing postulate. The four-object interface, settlement and provenance duties, sole-source faucet schedule and non-production assurance boundary are unchanged. The floor and controller belong above this imported burn interface; the development realization does not choose a floor implicitly or turn a faucet grant into a burn. Named obligations make evidence attributable without relying on list order.

**Amendment (Accepted burn amounts and evidence)** · `amend:devnet:marker-settlement` (`model:devnet:burn`), (`rule:devnet:burn-map`), (`rule:devnet:settlement`), (`rule:devnet:history`) and (`gate:devnet:implementation`) distinguish destruction evidence, lineage inclusion and network acceptance under ``post:comparator:marker-bound-settlement``. Publish the development marker policy identity and edition before genesis, fixed for that sequence, with open placement and cadence; admit its concrete representation only after independent conformance evidence. Whole-prefix lineage with accept-all is permitted as a declared special case, not inferred from block order or RPC arrival. The policy must recognize the initial marker with index zero, no predecessor and empty accepted set and snapshot, followed by unique immediate successors, require causal dependence on predecessor and lineage, recover complete accepted sets within lineage and above predecessor acceptance, and count each underlying destruction only once at its original address. A copied marker without its lineage, missing opening, invalid accepted record or mixed policy edition prevents recognition. Aggregating evidence is permitted when each original address and amount remain recoverable and unchanged; no aggregation moves or duplicates amount (``cond:comparator:burn-non-transferability``). A not-yet-final boundary may be lost on history replacement only subject to the last pinned amount floor (``cond:comparator:burn-revision-floor``); an admitted floor breach remains a run fault. Preserve marker evidence and acceptance facts in SCD2, and publish the complete accepted sets and evidence with every test epoch. Extend the same mock and real-chain suites from bite 2, with ledger, package and replay cases in their owning bites, including delayed acceptance, empty increments and mixed-history refusal. The declared unit-cost equality is specific to this profile's denomination and terms, and separate event costs are audit evidence, under ``cond:comparator:burn-unit-cost-projection``. The finite initial stock is the declared pre-opening bound. The faucet opens at a published boundary after the run starts and after the declared nonempty pre-opening interval, so the empty-interval zero case does not arise for this profile under ``cond:comparator:burn-bootstrap-capacity``. Publish applicable operator proof or explained absence under ``cond:comparator:burn-operator-audit-bound``. Neither chain settlement nor this conditional snapshot contract promises eventual or timed acceptance; production assurance remains excluded (`cav:devnet:assurance`).
